e.\par \par }{\f208\lang1036 10.}{\f208 \emdash }{\f208\lang1036  }{\f208 I, you[sg.], he.\par \par 11.\emdash  To-me, to-you[sg.], to-him.\par \par }{\b\f210 NOTES:\par }{\f208 1. }{\b\f210 V}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nio}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I come}{\f208 . E}{\b\f210 go}{\f208  }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nio}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 *I* come}{\f208 . As the form of the verb is different for each person, it is not necessary to specify it by a pronoun (}{\i\f211 I}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 you}{\f208 , etc.) to know }{\i\f211 who}{\f208  performs the action. Nonetheless, if this pronoun is used, it is quite simply because one wants to draw special attention to the identity of the one who performs the action: }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nio}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I come}{\f208  (without further specification), e}{\b\f210 go v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nio}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 *I* come}{\f208 , and I specify that it is definitely }{\i\f211 me}{\f208 , and not another, who comes; in English: }{\i\f211 *I* come}{\f208 , or: }{\i\f211 it is I who comes}{\f208 .\par 2. }{\b\f210 M}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 hi}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to me}{\f208 , just as in English one says: }{\i\f211 I}{\f208  give, give }{\i\f211 me}{\f208 , etc., in the same way, in Latin, the pronoun changes form according to whether it is a subject (performs the action), direct object (undergoes the action), indirect object (receives the effects of the action), etc. It is said that in the two languages, the pronoun is }{\i\f211 declined}{\f208 . We will see that in Latin it is not only the pronoun which is declined; little by little this concept of declension will become very natural to you.\par \par \par }{\i\f211 Do not forget to repeat each sentence aloud, initially while looking at the text, then without looking at it.\par }{\f208 \par Before closing the book, reread this lesson once or twice again and also reread the first lesson.\par \par If you have the audio recordings, do as you did for the first lesson and end y